            <ArticleTitle>Nicorandil associated anal ulceration.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>330-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Nicorandil is a vasodilator used to control angina. It has been associated with oral and anal ulceration that resolves upon withdrawal of the drug.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report a series of 13 patients with nonspecific anal ulceration all of whom were receiving nicorandil for control of symptoms of ischaemic heart disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All the ulcers had similar clinical and histological appearances. All the ulcers healed on withdrawal of the drug.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Nicorandil-associated anal ulceration should be considered in the differential diagnoses of nonhealing anal ulcers.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
